Report: GraphQL introspection is enabled on the authenticated endpoint /graphql. It exposes the mutation updateUserRole and the input type RoleUpdateInput. The validation for the role field appears to be implemented on the named argument rather than on the resolved input field, so a crafted mutation that changes the argument name, uses an alias, or sends the input object in a different key order may bypass the allowed-role check and let a non-admin assign OWNER to a user. Severity: high, pre-auth horizontal/vertical privilege escalation risk. Affected component: GraphQL resolver for updateUserRole plus introspection setting. Recommendation: disable introspection outside development; enforce role validation at the resolver or business-logic layer using the resolved input, not the raw argument name; add a schema allowlist and rate limit mutations; re-run the passive scan after fix.
I am not attempting the mutation, not constructing a proof-of-concept, and not altering any data. The finding is reported only.
